    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This report investigates the People's Republic of China's (PRC) covert information operations (IO) ecosystem, emphasizing the growing role of private firms in executing Foreign Information Manipulation and Interference (FIMI). It identifies 16 commercial entities linked to the PRC’s FIMI campaigns, providing services from content production to influencer management. The PRC's strategy, inspired by Russian tactics, aims to exploit societal fault lines, particularly targeting Taiwan to polarize its society and undermine democratic institutions. Despite Taiwan’s robust civil society efforts, gaps in coordination and an overemphasis on fact-checking over attribution hinder effective countermeasures. The report proposes forming a multisector FIMI attribution community to enhance accountability and policy enforcement, leveraging insights from the cyber threat intelligence community. It recommends increased funding for independent journalism, policy reforms to curb domestic and foreign information manipulation, and improved collaboration between governments and social media platforms to combat the PRC’s increasingly sophisticated IO tactics. The findings underscore the necessity for a global, coordinated response to address the challenges posed by PRC’s privatized FIMI operations.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Friis, G., Quak, N., Shah, S., and Stewart, E. (2024). Countering China’s Use of Private Firms in Covert Information Operations. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/fg865kf5598. https://doi.org/10.25740/fg865kf5598.",
